nodecode-slash-router is a rewrite of raitoxlol's raitoxlol/hermes-slash-router:

  https://github.com/raitoxlol/hermes-slash-router
  commit 2088919f4c59927aa2d47f21bb12e639453c1a2a
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 raitoxlol (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the routing itself, as Lisp verbs instead of a desktop
plugin. The original watched the Hermes Desktop composer, took a slash token
that was not an exact command, and asked TypeSafe's Jev -- one choice question
over the live command catalog -- to name the command it meant, acting only at
a confidence of 0.85 or more and re-deciding every use. This cell keeps that
core: the same POST <base>/v1/systemone, the same question and rubric, the
same catalog normalization (names lowercased and de-duplicated, descriptions
cut at 1000, at most 1000 commands), the same abstention, the same
profile-and-catalog-scoped corrections kept as advice and never as answers,
and the same exact-name-or-alias pass-through that costs no lookup. The key
comes from the same place the original's backend read it from,
TYPESAFE_API_KEY, and also from auth.json's api_keys.typesafe, as a Nodecode
provider key is kept; base URL and model are the cell's section.

What was left out, and why: everything that was the Desktop's, not the
router's. There is no composer middleware to intercept -- in Nodecode the
model calls (slash-router:route "/modles") when a user's /command did not
run -- so the draft rewriting, the "What did you mean?" field and the status
bar indicator are gone, replaced by one /slash-router command that says where
routing stands and can check the key. Reminders live in one JSON file under
the Nodecode home instead of Desktop plugin storage, and the routing-history
export and the installer are not carried over. The catalog is Nodecode's own
live slash catalog (the commands a headless caller can run) rather than a
Desktop catalog, and a caller may pass a catalog of its own.
